A||N||7||rnoswal||S-10 iron duke crankshaft||||1||
Z||000000||rnoswal||12-16-2000||08:05 PM||||Okay, which S-10's do I look for to find the better crankshaft? How much better is it and should it fit in my 85 4 cyl Fiero without any modifications? If so what would they be?<P>Thanks<BR>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||12-17-2000||02:01 PM||||Try looking up both engines in a parts book and see if they take the same crank. Any crank parts book should at least narrow it down.<P>You'll have to use a pre 87 S10. Like Fiero, S10 got DIS in it for 87.||||reg||1
Z||000002||HYPTNOTiSE||12-18-2000||01:45 PM||||I had one for the longest time that i would have given to you. I threw it out 2 weeks ago. <P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rnoswal||12-18-2000||01:50 PM||||Hey Hypno,  So you were just trying to make me feel good by telling me that right?!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Do you know if they are a bolt in and could you see if there is a difference between the fiero iron duke crank and the S-10 crank?<P>Thanks<BR>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrossum||12-18-2000||04:57 PM||||85 to 93 s10 use the same crank as fiero. they aren't any stronger than the fiero crank. be careful of the 86 s10 crank. some are undersized from the factory. stay away from these............||||reg||10
Z||000005||88formula||12-18-2000||09:10 PM||||I have worked with allot of these motors, rebuilt one, and had a fiero with one. From all of my experience with these 2.5-liter four cylinder motors I have come to one very thought out conclusion. They are total pieces of junk and are not worthy of your time and money. Junk the weak Iron Duke and get something else before you learn what I did the hard way. <P>In all my experience I never had one that ran 10,000 miles without something going wrong. One of the things that used to piss me off about these motors was the way they ran. All three of my motors always seemed to run with an intermittent ignition skip and every one I have heard had that same intermittent skip at idle. I wish GM had put a motor in the fiero that would have brought more credibility to the fiero.   <P>But if you must know go here. <A HREF="http://www.gtsonline.com/krp/prod2.htm#crankshaft" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gtsonline.com/krp/prod2.htm#crankshaft</A>   <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004568.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/004568.html</A>  <P>This one should help out allot if you have not found it already. <A HREF="http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/IronDuke.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero2m4.homestead.com/IronDuke.html</A>  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 12-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||rnoswal||12-19-2000||10:55 PM||||It does seem that the 2.5 from all accounts is a piece of crap if you want to modify it to get some decent horsepower numbers. I have not personally experienced the miss you are talking about above 4000 rpms. These spark systems seem to put out massive voltage if the sparkplug gap is set at .065! For such a light car, it wouldn't take much of a horsepower gain to make it feel strong though. This engine has pretty good low end torque, but runs out of steam pretty early. Is there a picture anywhere that might show the difference in the cranks that all came in these 2.5's? Except the super expensive super duty crank. I'd put a V-8 in it before that! Any real life numbers out there from those of you who have tried upping the horse power in the 4 cyl? Cams, pistons and are the rods the same as any of the 5.7in. rods that come in the 350's? If I had a V-6, in it already then I would not be worrying about all this, but I don't, so help me please  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Russ ||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||12-20-2000||06:25 PM||||I did a search for you and I could not find a thing. I have heard that rumor about the S-10 crankshaft before but I have never seen any evidence.<P> My brother had a 91 Pontiac Grand AM with the Iron Duke and that one had 110 horsepower and a 5500 rpm redline. It also had balance shafts that made it somewhat smoother. Maybe you could just swap one of these motors for the one you have now. I bet it would be easy. It is probably one of the better Iron Dukes GM made. It was definitely better than the one in my 87 fiero I had. It had lots of torque and pulled better on the top end than the 87 Duke did. <P>A least this post will bring this subject back to the top so that someone here can answer your que<BR>||||reg||1
